{% set local_problem %}
<p>Here's what I found cleaning a vacation home at <a href="/recent-projects/">Goose Pond Colony</a> last spring:</p>

<p>The homeowner hadn't been up since October. The gutters had six months of debris compacted into what I can only describe as gutter concrete — leaves, pine needles, oak tassels, and roof grit all compressed and glued together by moisture. It came out in chunks. Two of the downspouts were completely blocked, one at the outlet and one 6 feet down in the underground connection.</p>

<p>Here's the part that cost real money: behind the back gutter, where debris had been holding moisture against the fascia all winter, the wood was soft. I could push my finger into it. That section needed replacement before we could do anything else — $400 for 12 feet of fascia, and that was catching it relatively early.</p>

<p>At <a href="/recent-projects/">Lake Guntersville</a>, debris doesn't just sit in your gutters. It marinates. The humidity keeps it wet, the wet promotes breakdown, and the breakdown turns organic matter into a moisture-holding sludge that accelerates every problem. Cleaning isn't optional here — it's protective maintenance.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>When we clean gutters, here's what actually happens:</p>

<p><strong>First pass:</strong> Hand-remove bulk debris from inside gutters. Scoop it out, bag it, don't push it down the downspouts. This takes 80% of the time on most jobs.</p>

<p><strong>Second pass:</strong> Flush gutters with water to remove fine grit and test drainage. You'd be surprised how much sediment accumulates — roof granules, decomposed organic matter, dirt. If it doesn't flush clean, we find out why.</p>

<p><strong>Downspout flush:</strong> Every downspout gets high-pressure water through it. This clears partial clogs that homeowners miss because "water's still draining." A 50% blocked downspout will eventually become 100% blocked. We find those before they fail.</p>

<p><strong>Underground connections:</strong> If your downspouts tie into buried drains (common at lake properties), we flush those too. This is where a lot of clogs actually form — the transition from vertical downspout to horizontal underground pipe is a debris trap.</p>

<p><strong>Inspection:</strong> While we're up there, we check hanger tightness (they should be every 24" max, 18" is better), seam condition, and fascia health. We press on fascia boards to check for soft spots — the early warning sign of rot that you can't see from the ground.</p>

<p><strong>Documentation:</strong> For vacation homeowners, photos of everything. What we found, what we did, what needs attention. You'll see exactly what's happening at your property.</p>

<p>All debris leaves with us. We don't blow it onto your lawn or dump it behind the bushes.</p>
{% endset %}

